{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"one-insert-per-key-after-a-truncate","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One insert per key after a truncate"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pre-repair executable revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"da4e6b7cd"},{"type":"text","value":" (oracle extension on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"201220b51"},{"type":"text","value":").\nRepair executable revisions: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"67b5f73a1"},{"type":"text","value":", then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"64da25f00"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"law","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Law"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each change message must be valid for a consumer that has applied every\nearlier message: an insert names an absent key, and an update or delete names\na present one. Authority: the change-message contract in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/change-event-history-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and issue #1901. A\ntruncate batch is no exception. After its delete prefix, it must insert each\nvisible key at most once."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"gap","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Gap"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A truncate commit re-applies the optimistic upserts and publishes each one as\nan insert. The reapply loop in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" meant to replace the server's insert\nfor the same key. It ran before the changed-key loop that pushes that insert,\nso its search never matched. When a key was both re-applied and changed by\nthe commit, the batch inserted it two times."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optimistic-history oracle missed it because its only subscriber used\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeInitialState: true"},{"type":"text","value":". That subscription filters keys it already sent,\nso the second insert never reached the replica. The change-event oracle has a\nstrict mirror but uses a local-only Collection, which never truncates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"CodeRabbit found it on PR #2004. The state-stack mutation round 2 had already\nlisted the redundant insert push as reached in 72 cases on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":", but no\nobservation showed it there."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"repair-and-witnesses","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Repair and witnesses"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optimistic-history driver adds a raw subscriber that starts from the\nvisible rows. Its batches pass the same event-semantics check as the main\nsubscriber, and its replica must equal the model at each checkpoint."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The changed-key loop skips keys that the truncate already published."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-sync-reentrancy.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" pinned the duplicate in its trace\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[1, 2, 3, 1, 3, 1, 2]"},{"type":"text","value":". The trace is now "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[1, 2, 3, 1, 3, 2]"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"follow-up-from-review-64da25f00","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Follow-up from review: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"64da25f00"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ready callback during a truncate."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A truncate marks the Collection ready\nbefore its changed-key loop runs. A ready callback that edits a replaced key\nadds an optimistic upsert that the batch has not published. The first repair\nread the live upsert map, so it skipped that key's insert, and the subscriber\nlost the row. The loop now skips only the keys that the reapply loop\npublished, frozen before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markReady"},{"type":"text","value":". CodeRabbit found this on #2005. The\noptimistic-history grammar has no ready callbacks, so it could not reach this\nhistory. A witness in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collection-sync-reentrancy.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" covers\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFirstReady"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status:change"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks. It fails on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"67b5f73a1"},{"type":"text","value":" and\npasses on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"64da25f00"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accepted delete under an active insert."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A random campaign of the extended\noracle found a second invalid insert on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":", with no truncate. A completed\ndirect delete retires on the next sync commit. When an active optimistic\ninsert covered the key, retirement added the key to the changed keys without\nthe row that subscribers last saw, so the commit inserted it again. Upsert\nretirement already recorded that row. Both loops now share one helper. A\npinned replay in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"optimistic-history-publication-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" fails on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"main"},{"type":"text","value":" for\nan immediate commit. A non-immediate commit waits for the active insert, so\nit passes on both."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-outcomes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC outcomes"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002 independent judgment: the replica check uses the model's visible rows\nand the protocol rule, not production event code."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006 checker calibration: on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"da4e6b7cd"},{"type":"text","value":" the extended oracle fails 7 cases\nin the retention oracle, the settlement replays, and both random campaigns.\nA wrong repair that skips every changed key during a truncate fails 2 cases."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007 fixed and random campaigns: the fixed and random campaigns pass on\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"64da25f00"},{"type":"text","value":", including three runs at 20 times the usual count. The\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":" suite passes 236 files and 8,178 tests."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The raw subscriber has no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"where"},{"type":"text","value":" clause and no ordering. Filtered and limited\nraw subscriptions through a truncate remain with the WHERE predicate\npublication owner, which lists truncate as outside its scope."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"one-insert-per-key-after-a-truncate","text":"One insert per key after a truncate","level":1},{"id":"law","text":"Law","level":2},{"id":"gap","text":"Gap","level":2},{"id":"repair-and-witnesses","text":"Repair and witnesses","level":2},{"id":"follow-up-from-review-64da25f00","text":"Follow-up from review: 64da25f00","level":2},{"id":"orc-outcomes","text":"ORC outcomes","level":2},{"id":"limits","text":"Limits","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# One insert per key after a truncate\n\nPre-repair executable revision: `da4e6b7cd` (oracle extension on `201220b51`).\nRepair executable revisions: `67b5f73a1`, then `64da25f00`.\n\n## Law\n\nEach change message must be valid for a consumer that has applied every\nearlier message: an insert names an absent key, and an update or delete names\na present one. Authority: the change-message contract in\n`packages/db/tests/change-event-history-oracle.test.ts` and issue #1901. A\ntruncate batch is no exception. After its delete prefix, it must insert each\nvisible key at most once.\n\n## Gap\n\nA truncate commit re-applies the optimistic upserts and publishes each one as\nan insert. The reapply loop in `state.ts` meant to replace the server's insert\nfor the same key. It ran before the changed-key loop that pushes that insert,\nso its search never matched. When a key was both re-applied and changed by\nthe commit, the batch inserted it two times.\n\nThe optimistic-history oracle missed it because its only subscriber used\n`includeInitialState: true`. That subscription filters keys it already sent,\nso the second insert never reached the replica. The change-event oracle has a\nstrict mirror but uses a local-only Collection, which never truncates.\n\nCodeRabbit found it on PR #2004. The state-stack mutation round 2 had already\nlisted the redundant insert push as reached in 72 cases on `main`, but no\nobservation showed it there.\n\n## Repair and witnesses\n\n- The optimistic-history driver adds a raw subscriber that starts from the\n  visible rows. Its batches pass the same event-semantics check as the main\n  subscriber, and its replica must equal the model at each checkpoint.\n- The changed-key loop skips keys that the truncate already published.\n- `collection-sync-reentrancy.test.ts` pinned the duplicate in its trace\n  `[1, 2, 3, 1, 3, 1, 2]`. The trace is now `[1, 2, 3, 1, 3, 2]`.\n\n## Follow-up from review: `64da25f00`\n\n**Ready callback during a truncate.** A truncate marks the Collection ready\nbefore its changed-key loop runs. A ready callback that edits a replaced key\nadds an optimistic upsert that the batch has not published. The first repair\nread the live upsert map, so it skipped that key's insert, and the subscriber\nlost the row. The loop now skips only the keys that the reapply loop\npublished, frozen before `markReady`. CodeRabbit found this on #2005. The\noptimistic-history grammar has no ready callbacks, so it could not reach this\nhistory. A witness in `collection-sync-reentrancy.test.ts` covers\n`onFirstReady` and `status:change` callbacks. It fails on `67b5f73a1` and\npasses on `main` and `64da25f00`.\n\n**Accepted delete under an active insert.** A random campaign of the extended\noracle found a second invalid insert on `main`, with no truncate. A completed\ndirect delete retires on the next sync commit. When an active optimistic\ninsert covered the key, retirement added the key to the changed keys without\nthe row that subscribers last saw, so the commit inserted it again. Upsert\nretirement already recorded that row. Both loops now share one helper. A\npinned replay in `optimistic-history-publication-oracle.test.ts` fails on `main` for\nan immediate commit. A non-immediate commit waits for the active insert, so\nit passes on both.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\n- ORC-002 independent judgment: the replica check uses the model's visible rows\n  and the protocol rule, not production event code.\n- ORC-006 checker calibration: on `da4e6b7cd` the extended oracle fails 7 cases\n  in the retention oracle, the settlement replays, and both random campaigns.\n  A wrong repair that skips every changed key during a truncate fails 2 cases.\n- ORC-007 fixed and random campaigns: the fixed and random campaigns pass on\n  `64da25f00`, including three runs at 20 times the usual count. The\n  `@tanstack/db` suite passes 236 files and 8,178 tests.\n\n## Limits\n\nThe raw subscriber has no `where` clause and no ordering. Filtered and limited\nraw subscriptions through a truncate remain with the WHERE predicate\npublication owner, which lists truncate as outside its scope.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# One insert per key after a truncate\n\nPre-repair executable revision: `da4e6b7cd` (oracle extension on `201220b51`).\nRepair executable revisions: `67b5f73a1`, then `64da25f00`.\n\n## Law\n\nEach change message must be valid for a consumer that has applied every\nearlier message: an insert names an absent key, and an update or delete names\na present one. Authority: the change-message contract in\n`packages/db/tests/change-event-history-oracle.test.ts` and issue #1901. A\ntruncate batch is no exception. After its delete prefix, it must insert each\nvisible key at most once.\n\n## Gap\n\nA truncate commit re-applies the optimistic upserts and publishes each one as\nan insert. The reapply loop in `state.ts` meant to replace the server's insert\nfor the same key. It ran before the changed-key loop that pushes that insert,\nso its search never matched. When a key was both re-applied and changed by\nthe commit, the batch inserted it two times.\n\nThe optimistic-history oracle missed it because its only subscriber used\n`includeInitialState: true`. That subscription filters keys it already sent,\nso the second insert never reached the replica. The change-event oracle has a\nstrict mirror but uses a local-only Collection, which never truncates.\n\nCodeRabbit found it on PR #2004. The state-stack mutation round 2 had already\nlisted the redundant insert push as reached in 72 cases on `main`, but no\nobservation showed it there.\n\n## Repair and witnesses\n\n- The optimistic-history driver adds a raw subscriber that starts from the\n  visible rows. Its batches pass the same event-semantics check as the main\n  subscriber, and its replica must equal the model at each checkpoint.\n- The changed-key loop skips keys that the truncate already published.\n- `collection-sync-reentrancy.test.ts` pinned the duplicate in its trace\n  `[1, 2, 3, 1, 3, 1, 2]`. The trace is now `[1, 2, 3, 1, 3, 2]`.\n\n## Follow-up from review: `64da25f00`\n\n**Ready callback during a truncate.** A truncate marks the Collection ready\nbefore its changed-key loop runs. A ready callback that edits a replaced key\nadds an optimistic upsert that the batch has not published. The first repair\nread the live upsert map, so it skipped that key's insert, and the subscriber\nlost the row. The loop now skips only the keys that the reapply loop\npublished, frozen before `markReady`. CodeRabbit found this on #2005. The\noptimistic-history grammar has no ready callbacks, so it could not reach this\nhistory. A witness in `collection-sync-reentrancy.test.ts` covers\n`onFirstReady` and `status:change` callbacks. It fails on `67b5f73a1` and\npasses on `main` and `64da25f00`.\n\n**Accepted delete under an active insert.** A random campaign of the extended\noracle found a second invalid insert on `main`, with no truncate. A completed\ndirect delete retires on the next sync commit. When an active optimistic\ninsert covered the key, retirement added the key to the changed keys without\nthe row that subscribers last saw, so the commit inserted it again. Upsert\nretirement already recorded that row. Both loops now share one helper. A\npinned replay in `optimistic-history-publication-oracle.test.ts` fails on `main` for\nan immediate commit. A non-immediate commit waits for the active insert, so\nit passes on both.\n\n## ORC outcomes\n\n- ORC-002 independent judgment: the replica check uses the model's visible rows\n  and the protocol rule, not production event code.\n- ORC-006 checker calibration: on `da4e6b7cd` the extended oracle fails 7 cases\n  in the retention oracle, the settlement replays, and both random campaigns.\n  A wrong repair that skips every changed key during a truncate fails 2 cases.\n- ORC-007 fixed and random campaigns: the fixed and random campaigns pass on\n  `64da25f00`, including three runs at 20 times the usual count. The\n  `@tanstack/db` suite passes 236 files and 8,178 tests.\n\n## Limits\n\nThe raw subscriber has no `where` clause and no ordering. Filtered and limited\nraw subscriptions through a truncate remain with the WHERE predicate\npublication owner, which lists truncate as outside its scope.\n"}
